n $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=0$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-z=0-0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1-y=1-0=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-z=\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1-y=1-0=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈1.\n$$" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331108 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331109 13194139535100 true] [17592186331108 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331110 13194139535100 true] [17592186331108 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535100 true] [17592186331108 1041 "field-1" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331108 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331110 13194139535100 true] [17592186331108 1110 #uuid "f90ba6d9-f07e-571e-844f-ecef39b7a850" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331109 1034 "1" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331109 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535100 true] [17592186331109 1111 #uuid "bad61c0f-3658-5a70-8f4b-7766e7749d6b" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331110 1034 "0" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331110 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535100 true] [17592186331110 1111 #uuid "f4bcd3d5-2f34-5c45-ad50-83a3b821d19d" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331111 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331112 13194139535100 true] [17592186331111 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535100 true] [17592186331111 1041 "field-2" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331111 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331112 13194139535100 true] [17592186331111 1110 #uuid "eac7eead-390a-5174-84f4-1e0cc00753f7" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331112 1034 "1" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331112 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535100 true] [17592186331112 1111 #uuid "b5f01b1d-7d3e-5656-ae5f-de13364453f9" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186331114 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186331116 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1017 #edb/ref 17592186331118 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1089 "q-335288" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x),w=w(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=xy+z,\\, & y\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x+w,\\, & z\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=3 \\\\ {w}^{′}=-yz,\\, & w\\,(\\frac{2}{3})=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ $z(1),$ and $w(1).$\n\n$\\,y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}},\n$\\,w(1)≈$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1113 #uuid "fa558c70-a23d-5fa2-a0c3-715ff4f898df" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331113 1145 "Euler's method approximates the solution of an initial value problem containing a system of ODEs by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′},$ ${z}^{′},$ and ${w}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-1)+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+w \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ {w}^{′} & =-yz \\\\  & =-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  | $3$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  | $2$ | $3$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy,$ $Δz,$ and $Δw$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ Δw & ={w}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{7}{9}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{8}{9}$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ $z,$ and $w$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ $Δz$ to $z,$ and $Δw$ to $w,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{7}{9}=-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+\\frac{8}{9}=\\frac{35}{9} \\\\ {w}_{\\text{new}} & =w+Δw=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ | $w$ | ${w}^{′}$ | $Δw$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{7}{9}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{8}{3}$ | $\\frac{8}{9}$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{2}{9}$ |  |  | $\\frac{35}{9}$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈-\\frac{2}{9},\\,z(1)≈\\frac{35}{9},\\,w(1)≈3.\n$$" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331114 1000 #edb/ref 17592186331115 13194139535100 true] [17592186331114 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535100 true] [17592186331114 1041 "field-1" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331114 1104 #edb/ref 17592186331115 13194139535100 true] [17592186331114 1110 #uuid "67dadcbd-193e-5999-8a52-fc087d45014c" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331115 1034 "-\\frac{2}{9}" 13194139535100 true] [17592186331115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535100